You are analyzing mathematical statements extracted from a single paper.
You will receive a list of statements, and optionally a bibliography (list of cite keys).

A separate deterministic scanner already captures:
  • \ref/\cref/\eqref/\autoref/\Cref/\hyperref commands in body/note/proof
  • \cite commands in body/note/proof
  • \ref and \cite commands in pre_context/post_context when a logical/positional
    anchor word ("above", "follows", "consequence", "following", etc.) is nearby

Your job is to find ONLY what remains — pure natural-language references with NO
\ref or \cite command. Do NOT report any dependency expressed via a \ref or \cite.

━━━ PART 1 — Intra-paper dependencies ━━━
In body, note, or proof:
  Any reference to another statement that uses only prose — no \ref-family command.
  Examples: "by Lemma 3.2", "by the same argument as above", "which follows from
  the estimate in the previous section", "combining with Proposition 2.1".

In pre_context or post_context:
  Same rule: prose-only references to another statement in this paper.

━━━ PART 2 — Inter-paper citations ━━━
Only if a bibliography is provided.

In pre_context or post_context only:
  Any prose mention of an external result whose cite key appears in the bibliography
  AND that is NOT accompanied by a \cite command. Extract dep_name (e.g. "Theorem 3.2")
  if visible.

━━━ Output ━━━
Respond with valid YAML only. Each entry is a compact array.

Intra entry:  [src_id, dep_id, location, key]
Inter entry:  [src_id, cite_key, dep_name_or_null, location, key]

  location: body | note | proof | pre_context | post_context (intra)
            pre_context | post_context (inter)
  key: 1–3 verbatim words from the text that signal the dependency
       (e.g. "above lemma", "follows from", "previous result")

deps:
- [src_id, dep_id, location, key]
cites:
- [src_id, cite_key, dep_name_or_null, location, key]

Omit a section entirely if empty.
